● Clean build. Here's the full picture of what was done:

  Data flow:
  External TCP (148.113.204.83:8081)
    → xray dokodemo-door inbound (netmaker-api-in)
    → xray routing rule → netmaker-api-out (freedom redirect 127.0.0.1:18081)
    → incus proxy device on wg-xray (bind=container, listen=127.0.0.1:18081 →
  unix:/run/netmaker/api.sock on host)
    → incus proxy device on netmaker container
  (listen=unix:/run/netmaker/api.sock → connect=tcp:127.0.0.1:8081)
    → netmaker API (loopback only, no NIC)

  What changed:
  - plugin_schema_defs.rs — unix_socket_plugin_schema() example now declares all
  4 netmaker sockets; incus_plugin_schema() example declares all 3 netmaker
  containers (no NIC, proxy devices only) and the updated wg-xray definition
  with netmaker-socks disk + 4 TCP proxy devices
  - default_registry.rs — netmaker added to default_auto_load
  - /etc/xray/config.json — 4 new inbounds + 4 new TCP-redirect outbounds +
  routing rules, validated OK
  - wg-xray container — netmaker-socks disk device + 4 container-side TCP proxy
  devices applied live

  The IncusPlugin.apply_state will call sync_devices to install proxy devices on
  the netmaker containers when they're created. The UnixSocketPlugin reads the
  schema example from /dev/shm/live-schema.json and reconciles the socket files,
  which is what shows up in the control panel GUI.

